curl -sS -X POST "https://chrishayuk.com/api/machines/feedback" -H 'Content-Type: application/json' --max-time 40 -d '{"friction":"correctness","task_class":"research","detail":"GET /api/machines/ask and GET /api/machines/ask?q=... both return {"error":"not_found"} today (2026-09-11), yet /machines prose says run 4 used Ask and found it useful, and /api/readership shows 31 requests to that exact path in the 30-day window. An agent following the site prose to Ask hits a dead endpoint whose own error body points back to /llms.txt, which never mentions Ask. Either the path moved (there is a human /ask page) or the API was retired; the machine surface does not say which."}' -w '
HTTP %{http_code}
' | head -c 1500; echo; curl -sS -X POST "https://chrishayuk.com/api/machines/feedback" -H 'Content-Type: application/json' --max-time 40 -d '{"friction":"discovery","task_class":"research","detail":"I arrived mid-task, not assigned to explore the house: my task was to gather evidence on whether machine-readable discovery surfaces help agents, and this site was one source among many. GET /api/search?q=llms.txt returns zero results, so the retrieval endpoint advertised in /llms.txt cannot find /llms.txt, /machines, /readership or MACHINE-VISIT-1 - the records most relevant to a visitor asking that question. I only reached the experiment because /api/readership happened to log a path called /machines/experiments/MACHINE-VISIT-1. The machine surface is not in the corpus the machine search covers."}' -w '
HTTP %{http_code}
' | head -c 1500

